@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "SQL Server Data Types";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->
    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SQL Server Data Types
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            SQL Server data types define the kind of data that can be stored
            in a column, variable or expression. Choosing the correct data
            type helps SQL Server store data efficiently and maintain data
            accuracy.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT ARE DATA TYPES -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What are Data Types?
        </h2>

        <p>
            A data type specifies what kind of value can be stored in a
            database column or variable. For example, a student's name can
            be stored using a character data type, while a student's age
            can be stored using a numeric data type.
        </p>

        <p>
            Every column in a SQL Server table has a data type. The data
            type determines the type of values that the column can contain.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- WHY DATA TYPES ARE IMPORTANT -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Why are Data Types Important?
        </h2>

        <p>
            Selecting the appropriate data type is important because it
            affects storage, performance and the type of values that can
            be stored in a column.
        </p>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Helps maintain data accuracy.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Controls the type of values stored in a column.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Helps use database storage efficiently.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Makes database design more organized.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- DATA TYPE FLOW -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            How Data Types Work
        </h2>

        <p>
            When creating a table, we define a data type for every column.
            SQL Server then uses that definition when storing and processing
            the data.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL Server
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>
                                                                Column
                                                                   ↓
                                                                Data Type
                                                                   ↓
                                                                Allowed Values
                                                                   ↓
                                                                Stored Data
            </code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DATA TYPE CATEGORIES -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SQL Server Data Type Categories
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL Server provides different categories of data types for
            storing different kinds of information.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Category</th>
                        <th>Used For</th>
                        <th>Examples</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>Numeric</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Numbers and mathematical values
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            int, bigint, decimal, float
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>Character & String</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Text and character data
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            char, varchar, nchar, nvarchar
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>Date & Time</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Date and time values
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            date, datetime, time
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>Binary</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Binary data
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            binary, varbinary
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>Other</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Special types of data
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            bit, uniqueidentifier, xml
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- COMMON DATA TYPES -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common SQL Server Data Types
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Data Type</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>int</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores integer numbers.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            100
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>varchar</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores variable-length character data.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            'Rahul'
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>nvarchar</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores variable-length Unicode character data.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            N'Rahul'
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>decimal</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores exact numeric values.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            12500.50
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>date</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores date values.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            '2026-10-02'
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>bit</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Stores Boolean-like values.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            0 / 1
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- EXAMPLE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example: Using Data Types
        </h2>

        <p>
            The following example creates a Students table using different
            SQL Server data types.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    Age INT,
    Fees DECIMAL(10,2),
    BirthDate DATE,
    IsActive BIT
);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            Here, each column uses a data type suitable for the kind of
            information that it stores.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DATA TYPE SELECTION -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Choosing the Correct Data Type
        </h2>

        <p>
            The data type should be selected according to the kind of data
            that the column needs to store.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Requirement</th>
                        <th>Suitable Data Type</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Student ID</td>
                        <td><strong>INT</strong></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Student Name</td>
                        <td><strong>VARCHAR / NVARCHAR</strong></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Course Fees</td>
                        <td><strong>DECIMAL</strong></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Date of Birth</td>
                        <td><strong>DATE</strong></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Active / Inactive Status</td>
                        <td><strong>BIT</strong></td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT NOTE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Important
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                Always choose a data type based on the actual data that
                needs to be stored. Using an inappropriate data type can
                lead to unnecessary storage usage or unexpected results.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->
    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned what SQL Server data types are,
                why they are important and the major categories of data
                types. We also looked at common data types and how to choose
                an appropriate data type when designing a table.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>



